    "updated_at": "2022-01-12T22:00:11Z",
    "body": "I just ran into the \"lightweight tags aren't showing\" issue. A quick skim of the code shows it calling something called `peel_to_tag()?`, which I'm guessing is basically equivalent to the CLI's `tagname^{tag}` syntax, and which presumably fails on lightweight tags.\r\n\r\nHere's what I want to see:\r\n\r\n* By default, show lightweight tags. Prioritize any annotated tags first before lightweight tags.\r\n* Perhaps a config option to hide lightweight tags. I'm not sure.\r\n* I'm also tempted to say there's a use-case here for consulting the _repo's git config_ for settings relating to this stuff, so e.g. I could have different behavior in repos that have different tagging strategies (e.g. if one repo uses lightweight tags for releases I want to show those; if another uses annotated tags for releases but contains a bunch of random lightweight tags I might want to hide those). The idea of consulting the repo's config for this is orthogonal, but worth considering.\r\n* Any idea about showing tags for non-detached repos needs to have a config flag. I can see the potential utility but I'm also not sure I want that as my prompt is already pretty long.\r\n* I'm tempted to say there should be an option to hide the commit hash if it's showing a tag, again to combat the prompt length issue.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nSplitting `git_commit` into two modules seems ok as long as they can coordinate their display. I'm not all that fond of having my display look like `[ HEAD](ff2c90c)(🏷  foo)`. And I don't want to have to set my global `format` to include something like `[\\($git_commit$git_tag\\)](green bold)` (with corresponding changes to the per-module formats), especially as I've worked hard to keep it at `format = '$all'`.\r\n\r\nCould we just move the tag stuff into a sub-object within the `git_commit` configuration? This way we still configure it as its own object, but it can coordinate with the commit easily.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
